This project investigates the use of Wi-Fi Channel State Information (CSI) as a passive sensing mechanism for the detection and characterization of unmanned aerial vehicles (UAVs). By analyzing fine-grained variations in CSI caused by drone motion, rotor dynamics, and spatial interference with wireless signals, the system aims to identify the presence of drones without requiring active RF transmission from the UAV itself. Beyond basic detection, the project explores whether distinctive CSI signatures can be leveraged to estimate the number of drones present and to differentiate between drone types based on their physical and flight characteristics. Machine learning techniques are applied to extracted CSI features to enable classification and counting, with the goal of demonstrating a low-cost, infrastructure-based approach to drone detection that is suitable for real-world environments and complements existing RF- and vision-based counter-UAS systems.
